{
  "esm": "1.0.0",
  "metadata": {
    "name": "AgeDistribution",
    "description": "Stage 2(c) of the nr-logging-county chain: scrappage, equipment life and the base-year age distribution. Ports join J10 and the arithmetic of docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §2.2(c) (scrptime.f, crates/moves-nonroad/src/driver/scrptime.rs:84-176) for one equipment point.\n\nWHAT THIS FILE COMPUTES: the median life in years, the cumulative scrappage percent at each age, the YEAR-OVER-YEAR scrapped fraction derived from it, the equipment life `nyrlif`, the sales curve the population growth factor implies, the 1990 model-year fractions, and -- since F12 was fixed upstream -- agedist.f's thirty-year fold of those fractions to the episode year. Every one of those is checked against docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §6.1 steps 2 and 3, and the fold is checked on two further equipment points against §6.2.\n\nHOW THE FOLD IS SPELLED, AND WHAT IT USED TO SAY HERE. This paragraph used to say the fold was not computed: growing the 1990 distribution to 2020 is a RECURRENCE -- each year's vector is the previous year's shifted, scrapped and topped up with an unclamped residual -- and the format had no spelling for one (finding F12). F12 is FIXED (EarthSciAST a83cde55e) and the fold is computed here now. It is spelled as esm-spec §4.3.1.1's causal self-reference: `fold_scrappedFraction` is an array-shaped unknown whose own `aggregate` body reads `index(fold_scrappedFraction, Y - a)`, itself, strictly earlier along its own output axis, and the recurrence, its axis and its maximum lag of 50 are read off that one read. No new op, no new schema field, nothing declared.\n\nThe 51-slot vector fold is first reduced to a scalar recurrence on 31 residuals, which is what makes it expressible at all: every cell of the grown vector is a clamped base times an ascending run of survival factors, so slot `a` of year `Y` traces back `a` years to the residual of year `Y - a`, or to the 1990 vector if the fold has not run that long. That reduction rests on `1 - yryrfrcscrp >= 0` -- a non-negative cohort times a non-negative survival stays non-negative, so along any chain the clamp bites at most once -- and it is verified, not assumed: tools/verify-agedist-reduction.py checks it cell by cell in float32 against the specification's own script, 0 of 1,581 cells differing, on all six of the fixture's equipment points.\n\nTWO ORDERS THAT ARE NOT FREE, both measured rather than argued. (i) The residual is `minuend - sum`, with the cohorts summed in ascending slot order FIRST; folding it as one aggregate that adds the negated terms to the minuend one at a time moves the answer by up to 1.1e-05 relative, because the residual is a catastrophic cancellation. (ii) Each cohort's factors multiply left to right STARTING FROM the base, `((base * S[lo]) * S[lo+1]) * ...`; precomputing the run product and multiplying the base in afterwards moves it by up to 6.4e-06. Written as specified, all 306 cells of all six points reproduce the reference fold BIT-EXACTLY.\n\nTHE PRECISION IS DECLARED, AND IT HAS TO BE. This file evaluates in binary32 (`domain.element_type`), because the code it ports is `real*4` and because the answer differs: `age_survivalFraction` at age 3 is 5.96e-08 in binary32 and exactly 0 in binary64, and thirty fold iterations later that is the difference between model year 2018 of this SCC having four MOVESOutput rows and having none (§7.3). The file used to evaluate in binary64 and assert the zero while this description said 5.96e-08; the assertions now agree with the description. What it costs is the tolerance on the tests whose assertions are floating-point quantities, moved to 2^-23 per test as docs/esm-conventions.md §17.4 requires -- never the expected values, which stay the specification's decimals.\n\nTHE STEP FUNCTION IS A STEP FUNCTION. find_scrappage_percent (output/find.rs:233-244) returns the percent of the last breakpoint AT OR BELOW the life fraction — it does not interpolate between breakpoints, which is why the whole 197-point default curve is carried here rather than a handful of points around the answer: which breakpoint a life fraction lands on is the computation. The curve is the snapshot's `nrscrappagecurve` for NREquipTypeID 0, the only curve canonical writes.\n\nA NOTE ON `nyrlif`, RECONCILED WITH THE CODE. §6.3's prose says the 750-hour equipment point's `nyrlif` is 38; the code, and this file, give 39 — the first age whose cumulative scrappage reaches 100% is age 39, since (39-1) x (1/18.75) = 2.027 and (38-1) x (1/18.75) = 1.973. The difference costs nothing downstream because that age's grown fraction is not positive and the model year is absent from the snapshot either way, but the number here follows scrptime.rs:110-135 rather than the prose.",
    "authors": [
      "EarthSciML Authors and Contributors"
    ],
    "tags": [
      "moves",
      "nonroad",
      "population",
      "scrappage",
      "component"
    ]
  },
  "domain": {
    "element_type": "Float32"
  },
  "enums": {
    "scrappage": {
      "FullyScrappedPercent": 100
    }
  },
  "index_sets": {
    "age_rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 51
    },
    "scrappage_curve_rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 197
    },
    "fold_year_rows": {
      "kind": "interval",
      "size": 31
    }
  },
  "coordinates": {
    "age_model_year": {
      "source": "age_modelYearID",
      "standard_name": "model_year"
    }
  },
  "models": {
    "AgeDistribution": {
      "expression_template_imports": [
        {
          "ref": "../lib/population.esm",
          "only": [
            "max_equipment_ages",
            "median_life_years",
            "year_over_year_scrap_fraction",
            "scrappage_sales_growth",
            "surviving_equipment",
            "agedist_cohort_term"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tolerance": {
        "rel": 1e-12
      },
      "variables": {
        "hoursUsedPerYear": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "h/yr",
          "default": 303.0,
          "description": "nrsourceusetype.hoursUsedPerYear for sourceTypeID 1059 (SCC 2260007005), the §6.1 equipment point. Carried with its rate units here, unlike components/deteriorated_emission_rate.esm's plain `h`, because the median life below is only a number of YEARS if the annual usage is a rate."
        },
        "loadFactor": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.7,
          "description": "nrsourceusetype.loadFactor."
        },
        "medianLifeFullLoad": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "h",
          "default": 191.0,
          "description": "nrsourceusetype.medianLifeFullLoad, in full-load-equivalent hours."
        },
        "popGrowthFactor": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.40152301834544835,
          "description": "grwfac(1990, 1991) from components/growth_index.esm. NOT the growth over the whole projection: scrptime's sales curve is built from the FIRST year's rate (§2.2(c))."
        },
        "runYearID": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 2020.0,
          "description": "The episode year. Model year = runYearID - (age - 1)."
        },
        "curve_fractionLifeUsed": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "scrappage_curve_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "nrscrappagecurve.fractionLifeUsed for NREquipTypeID 0. Column 1: the breakpoint, in fractions of median life consumed."
        },
        "curve_percentageScrapped": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "scrappage_curve_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "nrscrappagecurve.percentageScrapped. Column 2: cumulative percent scrapped at that breakpoint, 0 at 0.0 and 100 at 2.0."
        },
        "age_ageID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "description": "1-based equipment age in years, 1 to MXAGYR. Age 1 is the newest cohort."
        },
        "age_modelYearID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The model year this age is, at the episode year: 2020 for age 1."
        },
        "age_fractionLifeUsed": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "(age - 1) / medianLifeYears: how much of the median life this cohort has consumed."
        },
        "age_priorFractionLifeUsed": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "The same for the age before it, floored at 0 so age 1's predecessor reads the curve's origin. It is computed rather than read from the neighbouring row because a relation cannot be joined to itself (docs/findings F11)."
        },
        "age_percentScrapped": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "Cumulative percent scrapped at this age: the curve's value at the last breakpoint AT OR BELOW age_fractionLifeUsed."
        },
        "age_priorPercentScrapped": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "The same at the previous age. scrptime.rs works with both at once, which is what makes the year-over-year fraction a difference of cumulatives rather than a curve of its own."
        },
        "age_yearScrapFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "yryrfrcscrp: the fraction of the cohort SURVIVING at the previous age that is scrapped during this one. Once the previous age is fully scrapped the entry is 0 rather than a division by zero (scrptime.rs:126-131)."
        },
        "age_survivalFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "1 - yryrfrcscrp: the factor agedist.f applies when it shifts a cohort one year older. §7.3 lives here — in float32 the age-3 entry of this equipment point is 5.96e-08 rather than 0, and those four output rows exist only because of it."
        },
        "age_survivingEquipment": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "The 1990 stock at this age: the sales of the year it was built, decayed by its cumulative scrappage. Ages beyond nyrlif carry 0."
        },
        "age_baseModelYearFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "modfrc before growth: the share of the 1990 population at this age. Sums to 1."
        },
        "fold_yearID": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "fold_year_rows"
          ],
          "description": "The calendar year each fold row stands for: baseYearID + (row - 1), so row 1 is 1990, the base distribution before any growth, and row 31 is 2020. The two equations that read the last row index it as 31; the assertion that this column's row 31 is 2020 is what guards that literal."
        },
        "fold_annualGrowthFactor": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "fold_year_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "grwfac(year-1, year) for each fold row, from components/growth_index.esm's `f_growthFactor` on growth pattern 2176 -- the same series that component computes from nrgrowthindex and the same carrying convention as `popGrowthFactor` above. Row 1 is grwfac(1989, 1990), which the fold never reads because row 1 is its base case; rows 2..31 are the thirty steps 1991..2020."
        },
        "fold_totalPopulation": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "fold_year_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "totpop: the projected population after this row's growth step, starting from basePopulation. A RECURRENCE -- each row is the previous row's value, floored at MINGRWIND when the factor is non-zero, grown, and clamped at zero (agedist.rs:133-136)."
        },
        "fold_residualMinuend": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "fold_year_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "The number the newest-model-year slot is the residual OF: `totpopfrc = totpop / basePopulation` at every fold row but the first, and the base vector's own newest-cohort fraction at the first. Carrying the base case here rather than in the recurrence is what lets `fold_newestCohortResidual = fold_residualMinuend - fold_scrappedFraction` hold at EVERY row, including row 1, so the recurrence below can read a lag of any size with one expression and no second base case."
        },
        "fold_scrappedFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "fold_year_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "frcsum: the sum over age slots 1..MXAGYR-1 of the cohorts that survived into this year, in ASCENDING slot order, which is the order agedist.f accumulates them in. THIS IS THE RECURRENCE (esm-spec §4.3.1.1): its body reads `fold_scrappedFraction` itself at row `Y - a`, strictly earlier along its own output axis, once per age slot `a`. Nothing is declared -- the recurrence, its axis and its maximum lag of 50 are read off that self-read. Row 1 is 0 by a guard inside the body, not by falling off the end of the axis, because a self-read of an unpublished cell faults rather than reading a zero ghost."
        },
        "fold_newestCohortResidual": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "fold_year_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "mdyrfrc[0] at each fold row: the minuend less the scrapped sum, UNCLAMPED. This is the quantity whose sign decides whether a model year exists at all -- when the truncated growth index dips year over year it goes negative, the next year's shift clamps it to zero, and prccty.f's `modfrc <= 0` skip drops the row (§2.2(e) property 2, §6.3). It is stated as its own column rather than folded into the fold so that the sign is assertable."
        },
        "age_grownModelYearFraction": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "shape": [
            "age_rows"
          ],
          "units": "1",
          "description": "modfrc after agedist.f's 1990->2020 fold: the last row of the fold, read back. COMPUTED HERE, from the base fractions, the year-over-year survivals and components/growth_index.esm's annual growth factors -- it used to be a `const` carrying docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §6.1 step 3's values, because the fold was a recurrence the format could not express (finding F12, now fixed). Those same values are what it is asserted against. They sum to G(2020)/G(1990) = 4.697819 rather than to 1, which is the check §2.2(e) gives for having folded the right number of years, and which components/growth_index.esm reaches independently by a single division."
        },
        "medianLifeYears": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "yr",
          "description": "min(25, (medianLifeFullLoad / loadFactor) / hoursUsedPerYear). The division order is scrptime.rs:102's and the cap is MXAGYR/2."
        },
        "medianLifePerYear": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "1 / medianLifeYears: the life fraction one year of use consumes."
        },
        "equipmentLifeYears": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "nyrlif: the first age at which cumulative scrappage reaches 100%, and so the number of model years this equipment point spans."
        },
        "salesGrowth": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "The sales trend scrptime.rs:139-143 infers from one year's population growth."
        },
        "survivingEquipmentTotal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "The normalizing sum of age_survivingEquipment."
        },
        "grownModelYearFractionTotal": {
          "type": "unknown",
          "units": "1",
          "description": "The sum of the grown fractions, which must equal the cumulative growth ratio."
        },
        "baseYearID": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 1990.0,
          "description": "NRBaseYearID: the year the population and the base age distribution are stated in, and the year the fold starts from. `fold_year_rows` spans runYearID - baseYearID + 1 rows, so the last row is the episode year."
        },
        "basePopulation": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 83.3,
          "description": "The .POP-rounded state population agedist.f folds -- 83.279223744292 rounded to 83.3 by the %17.1f of the .POP file (docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §6.1 step 1). It is NOT a scale on the answer: the fold divides the grown total by it again, so it enters only through that division's rounding and through the MINGRWIND clamp. Two equipment points of §6.3 differ in nothing else and their grown fractions still differ in the last bits, which is why it is a parameter rather than a 1."
        },
        "minimumGrowthPopulation": {
          "type": "parameter",
          "units": "1",
          "default": 0.0001,
          "description": "MINGRWIND (agedist.rs:135). A floor applied to the running total ONCE PER YEAR and only when that year's growth factor is non-zero, before the factor is applied. It cannot bind on any of this fixture's six points -- the smallest base population is 0.5 -- and it is carried because leaving it out would make the port agree by accident on this data and disagree on a state with almost no equipment."
        }
      },
      "equations": [
