        },
        {
          "_comment": "The thirty annual growth factors the fold applies, transcribed from components/growth_index.esm's `f_growthFactor` for pattern 2176 (its rows 1..30, years 1991..2020) exactly as `popGrowthFactor` above transcribes that component's first one. The leading 0.0 is row 1, the base year: the fold takes no step there, so the entry is never read, and there is no grwfac(1989, 1990) to write -- nrgrowthindex's pattern 2176 begins at 1990.",
          "lhs": "fold_annualGrowthFactor",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "const",
            "args": [],
            "value": [
              0.0,
              0.40152299404144287,
              0.2864904999732971,
              0.22269144654273987,
              0.18213225901126862,
              0.15407095849514008,
              0.1335020363330841,
              0.1177784875035286,
              0.10536836087703705,
              0.09532420337200165,
              -0.32478055357933044,
              -0.03500000014901161,
              0.2031088024377823,
              0.5004306435585022,
              0.0660160705447197,
              0.0700053870677948,
              -0.2159033715724945,
              -0.17137356102466583,
              -0.20371805131435394,
              -0.009727626107633114,
              0.0707269161939621,
              0.22844037413597107,
              0.09634055197238922,
              0.15190735459327698,
              -0.0780603215098381,
              0.006414368283003569,
              -0.018483109772205353,
              -0.009740259498357773,
              -0.005901639349758625,
              0.0006596306338906288,
              -0.005932761821895838
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "agedist.rs:133-136. A recurrence in its own right, and the simplest one in the file: lag 1, one term. The MINGRWIND floor is applied to the RUNNING TOTAL before the year's factor, and only when that factor is non-zero; the clamp at zero is applied after. Doing either the other way round is a different projection for a state whose equipment population is near zero.",
          "lhs": "fold_totalPopulation",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "Y"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "Y": {
                "from": "fold_year_rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "ifelse",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "<=",
                  "args": [
                    "Y",
                    1
                  ]
                },
                "basePopulation",
                {
                  "op": "max",
                  "args": [
                    {
                      "op": "*",
                      "args": [
                        {
                          "op": "ifelse",
                          "args": [
                            {
                              "op": "!=",
                              "args": [
                                {
                                  "op": "index",
                                  "args": [
                                    "fold_annualGrowthFactor",
                                    "Y"
                                  ]
                                },
                                0.0
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "op": "max",
                              "args": [
                                {
                                  "op": "index",
                                  "args": [
                                    "fold_totalPopulation",
                                    {
                                      "op": "-",
                                      "args": [
                                        "Y",
                                        1
                                      ]
                                    }
                                  ]
                                },
                                "minimumGrowthPopulation"
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "op": "index",
                              "args": [
                                "fold_totalPopulation",
                                {
                                  "op": "-",
                                  "args": [
                                    "Y",
                                    1
                                  ]
                                }
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "op": "+",
                          "args": [
                            1.0,
                            {
                              "op": "index",
                              "args": [
                                "fold_annualGrowthFactor",
                                "Y"
                              ]
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    0.0
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "lhs": "fold_residualMinuend",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "Y"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "Y": {
                "from": "fold_year_rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "ifelse",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "<=",
                  "args": [
                    "Y",
                    1
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "index",
                  "args": [
                    "age_baseModelYearFraction",
                    1
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "/",
                  "args": [
                    {
                      "op": "index",
                      "args": [
                        "fold_totalPopulation",
                        "Y"
                      ]
                    },
                    "basePopulation"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "agedist.f's thirty-year fold (§2.2(e)), as the causal self-reference of esm-spec §4.3.1.1. The LHS is an array-shaped unknown; the body reads `index(fold_scrappedFraction, Y - a)` -- itself, strictly earlier along its own output axis -- so the recurrence, its axis and its maximum lag are all derived from the read and nothing is declared. The contracted `a` is the age slot, 1..MXAGYR-1, and it is also the lag: slot `a` of year `Y` traces back `a` years, to the residual of year `Y - a` if the fold has run that long and to the 1990 vector otherwise. That reduction of the 51-slot vector fold to a scalar recurrence on 31 residuals is what tools/verify-agedist-reduction.py checks, cell by cell, in float32, on all six of the fixture's equipment points. The `a = 0` slot of the canonical spelling is not used here because the non-recurrent term is not additive: it is `fold_residualMinuend`, and it is subtracted FROM rather than added TO, which is why this fold sums the scrapped cohorts and takes the difference in a separate equation. Reversing that -- one aggregate accumulating `minuend + (-term_1) + (-term_2) + ...` -- is a different float32 number, measured at up to 1.1e-05 relative on a grown fraction, because the residual is a catastrophic cancellation: 4.6978183 - 0.9905498 leaves 5.9e-08 in slot 3 thirty years later.",
          "lhs": "fold_scrappedFraction",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "Y"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "Y": {
                "from": "fold_year_rows"
              },
              "a": [
                1,
                50
              ]
            },
            "reduce": "+",
            "expr": {
              "op": "ifelse",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "<=",
                  "args": [
                    "Y",
                    1
                  ]
                },
                0.0,
                {
                  "op": "apply_expression_template",
                  "args": [],
                  "name": "agedist_cohort_term",
                  "bindings": {
                    "fold_year": "Y",
                    "age_slot": "a",
                    "survival": "age_survivalFraction",
                    "base_fraction": "age_baseModelYearFraction",
                    "residual_minuend": "fold_residualMinuend",
                    "scrapped_sum": "fold_scrappedFraction"
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "lhs": "fold_newestCohortResidual",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "Y"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "Y": {
                "from": "fold_year_rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "-",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "index",
                  "args": [
                    "fold_residualMinuend",
                    "Y"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "index",
                  "args": [
                    "fold_scrappedFraction",
                    "Y"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "_comment": "The last year's vector, read back out of the fold. Slot 1 is the newest model year -- the unclamped residual -- and slot `a+1` is the cohort that has been shifted and scrapped `a` times. `31` is the last row of fold_year_rows, the episode year: `fold_yearID` row 31 is asserted to be 2020 so that the literal cannot drift away from the axis.",
          "lhs": "age_grownModelYearFraction",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [
              "a"
            ],
            "ranges": {
              "a": {
                "from": "age_rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "ifelse",
              "args": [
                {
                  "op": "<=",
                  "args": [
                    "a",
                    1
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "index",
                  "args": [
                    "fold_newestCohortResidual",
                    31
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "op": "apply_expression_template",
                  "args": [],
                  "name": "agedist_cohort_term",
                  "bindings": {
                    "fold_year": 31,
                    "age_slot": {
                      "op": "-",
                      "args": [
                        "a",
                        1
                      ]
                    },
                    "survival": "age_survivalFraction",
                    "base_fraction": "age_baseModelYearFraction",
                    "residual_minuend": "fold_residualMinuend",
                    "scrapped_sum": "fold_scrappedFraction"
                  }
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "lhs": "grownModelYearFractionTotal",
          "rhs": {
            "op": "aggregate",
            "args": [],
            "output_idx": [],
            "ranges": {
              "a": {
                "from": "age_rows"
              }
            },
            "expr": {
              "op": "index",
              "args": [
                "age_grownModelYearFraction",
                "a"
              ]
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "median_life_divides_left_to_right_and_caps_at_25",
          "description": "docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §6.1 step 2: min(25, (191/0.7)/303) = 0.90051866 years, so one year of use consumes 1.1104711 median lives. Grouping the division the other way, 191/(0.7*303), gives the same number here only because multiplication is associative in exact arithmetic -- the cap is what actually differs between ports, and at 25 years it binds for the long-lived equipment of §6.3. This document now evaluates in binary32 (`domain.element_type`), so this test carries the 2^-23 tolerance docs/esm-conventions.md §17.4 fixes for a test whose every assertion is a floating-point quantity: the expected values stay the specification's decimals and the tolerance moves, never the other way round.",
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 1.1920929e-07
          },
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "medianLifeYears",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.9005186232909006
            },
            {
              "variable": "medianLifePerYear",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 1.1104712041884817
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_scrappage_curve_is_stepped_not_interpolated",
          "description": "docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §6.1 step 2's table. At age 2 the life fraction is 1.1104711 and the curve's last breakpoint at or below it carries 73.5%; interpolating between neighbouring breakpoints would give about 73.7% and change every model-year fraction. At age 3 the fraction is 2.2209 and the curve has already reached 100%.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "age_percentScrapped",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "age_rows": 1
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "age_percentScrapped",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 73.5,
              "coords": {
                "age_rows": 2
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "age_percentScrapped",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 100.0,
              "coords": {
                "age_rows": 3
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "age_priorPercentScrapped",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 73.5,
              "coords": {
                "age_rows": 3
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "age_priorPercentScrapped",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.0,
              "coords": {
                "age_rows": 1
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_year_over_year_fraction_is_conditional_not_a_difference",
          "description": "scrptime.rs:122-131. Age 2 scraps 73.5% of what age 1 had; age 3 scraps ALL of what age 2 had, which is 100 x 0.265/26.5 = exactly 1 in binary64. Age 4 has nothing left, and the guard makes it 0 rather than 0/0. docs/nonroad-logging-county.md §7.3: in float32 the age-3 entry is 0.99999994, so the survival factor is 5.96e-08 instead of 0, and those four snapshot rows exist only because of that one ulp. THE AGE-3 SURVIVAL IS 5.96e-08, NOT 0, and that is the point of §7.3: `age_percentScrapped` reaches exactly 100 at age 3, so `age_yearScrapFraction` is 100 x 0.265 / 26.5, which is 0.99999994 in binary32 and exactly 1 in binary64. The residue is what carries the 2018 model year through thirty fold iterations and out into four MOVESOutput rows. This file used to assert the binary64 zero here while its own description said 5.96e-08; it now evaluates in the precision the reference does and asserts what that gives. This document now evaluates in binary32 (`domain.element_type`), so this test carries the 2^-23 tolerance docs/esm-conventions.md §17.4 fixes for a test whose every assertion is a floating-point quantity: the expected values stay the specification's decimals and the tolerance moves, never the other way round.",
